\section{Theoretical Analysis}
\label{sec:theory}

Sign misattribution is the more fundamental failure mechanism because it causes active divergence, not merely slow convergence. We derive the probability of incorrect gradient direction below; full proofs appear in Appendix~\ref{app:theory_full}.

\textbf{Definition 1} (Locally correct action). \textit{An action $a_t$ is \textbf{locally correct} if it would receive positive advantage under an oracle that observes the full episode history $h_t$ and has access to the true action-value function. Equivalently, window $k$ contains locally correct actions if replacing them (holding all other windows fixed) would decrease the probability of episode success.}

\textbf{Proposition 1.} \textit{Under step-independent training with binary episode reward $R_{\text{episode}} \in \{0, r_+\}$, the probability that window $k$'s advantage $\hat{A}_k$ has the correct sign (aligned with local action quality) approaches the base success rate $p$ as the number of rollouts $K$ per group increases.}

\textit{Proof sketch.} In a failed episode ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive $R_k = 0$. If any rollout in the group succeeded, $\mu > 0$, so every window in the failed episode receives $\hat{A}_k < 0$---including windows with locally correct actions. The probability of receiving the wrong sign in a failed episode is $1 - (1-p)^{K-1}$ (at least one group member succeeded). As $K$ grows, this approaches $1-p$, and the overall probability of correct sign approaches $p$. \hfill$\square$

The key distinction is temporal. Information loss (M1) imposes a constant ceiling determined by $h$; sign misattribution (M2) is cumulative---each wrong-sign gradient step erodes policy competence below that ceiling. A wrong-sign gradient moves the optimizer \textit{away} from good policies; a reduced-magnitude gradient from discounting ($\gamma^{(N-k)}$) still points toward good policies. Sign errors produce active divergence; magnitude errors merely slow convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:sign_fundamental}).

\textbf{Proposition 2.}\label{prop:correct_sign} \textit{Let $p$ denote the episode success rate and $K$ the number of rollouts per group. Under step-independent training with binary episode reward, the fraction of gradient updates with correct sign is:}
\begin{equation}
P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = \frac{p}{1 - (1-p)^{K}}
\label{eq:correct_conditional}
\end{equation}

\textit{Derivation.} Three mutually exclusive cases determine gradient direction for a window with locally correct actions: (1)~episode succeeded (probability $p$): correct sign; (2)~episode failed but group contains a success (probability $(1{-}p)[1{-}(1{-}p)^{K-1}]$): wrong sign; (3)~entire group failed (probability $(1{-}p)^K$): zero gradient. Conditioning on non-zero gradient (Cases 1 and 2) yields Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}.

At $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$, only 19.1\% of gradient updates have the correct sign (Figure~\ref{fig:theory_curve}). In the large-$K$ limit, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$: ${\sim}87.5\%$ of updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_theory_curve.pdf}
\caption{Probability of correct gradient sign as a function of episode success rate $p$ (Proposition~2). At typical agent task success rates ($p < 0.2$, shaded), the majority of gradient updates carry incorrect sign regardless of group size $K$. At ALFWorld's baseline ($p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$), only 19.1\% of updates are correct. The dashed diagonal shows the large-$K$ limit $P_{\text{correct}} \to p$.}
\label{fig:theory_curve}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Severity scales with task difficulty.} Sign misattribution becomes \textit{more} severe as the task gets harder (Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}): at $p{=}50\%$, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = 50.2\%$---barely above random; at $p{=}5\%$, only 5.1\% of updates are correct. Multi-turn agent tasks with low baseline success rates are precisely where credit assignment matters most, yet where step-independent training produces the worst gradient signal. Increasing $K$ does not help: more updates occur (fewer all-fail groups), but each remains equally likely to carry the wrong sign.

\paragraph{Reward saturation.} When all $K$ rollouts receive the same binary reward, $\sigma = 0$ and $\hat{A}_k = 0$, producing zero gradient. At $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$, the all-fail probability is $(1{-}p)^K \approx 34.4\%$; combined with the negligible all-success case ($p^K < 10^{-7}$), roughly one-third of batches yield no gradient at all. This compounds sign misattribution: of the ${\sim}66\%$ of batches that produce a gradient, only 19.1\% have the correct sign. The effective learning rate is $0.66 \times 0.19 \approx 12.5\%$ of nominal---well below the regime where convergence guarantees hold.

\paragraph{Cumulative divergence.} Sign misattribution produces a systematic gradient bias. Let $\theta^*$ denote an optimal policy parameterization. At each update, the expected gradient under step-independent training is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}] = P_c \cdot g_+ + (1 - P_c) \cdot g_-
\label{eq:biased_gradient}
\end{equation}
where $P_c = P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$, $g_+$ points toward $\theta^*$, and $g_-$ points away. Empirical measurement from training logs confirms approximately comparable per-group advantage magnitudes ($|\hat{A}_{\text{correct}}|/|\hat{A}_{\text{wrong}}| \approx 1.32$). When $P_c < 0.5$ (which holds for $p < 0.5$; Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}), the expected gradient points weakly away from good policies. The cumulative effect of this directional bias, amplified by the M1--M2 positive feedback loop (Section~\ref{sec:dual_mechanism}), drives progressive policy degradation: information loss (M1) caps $p$; lower $p$ increases wrong-sign frequency under M2, which further depresses $p$, producing the empirically observed collapse.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Step-independent training collapses while episode-level training succeeds; a factorial design separates the two mechanisms; and window size $h$ modulates severity.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment with six task types requiring sequential reasoning over up to 50 steps. Reward is binary: $R \in \{0, r_+\}$. The base policy is Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned on expert demonstrations (SFT baseline: 12.5\% in-distribution). At this baseline success rate, Proposition~2 predicts the majority of gradient updates carry incorrect sign. All conditions use RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} (v0.1, commit \texttt{3a6b48b})\footnote{Our implementation extends RAGEN v0.1 with episode-level training support. Code: \url{https://github.com/RAGEN-AI/RAGEN}.} with GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparameters}). We report success rate on a 134-task OOD set (primary) and 32-task in-distribution set (secondary). Seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

We implement the factorial design from Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design} with two seeds per condition. Cell~C (full observation, step-level advantage) would isolate M2 without M1, but we encountered persistent training instability in this configuration (Appendix~\ref{app:cell_c}).

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cell~A collapses in two phases (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): task success drops to 0\% while action validity initially remains high, then continued sign misattribution destroys even basic formatting.% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, hsweep_h2_seed2
Cell~B eliminates sign misattribution while retaining information loss: training success improves, but OOD generalization is bounded by the information-loss ceiling. Both seeds converge to the same OOD ceiling despite different training trajectories, confirming the bottleneck is architectural rather than statistical.% exp_id: factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed2

Cell~D is the only condition that substantially exceeds the SFT baseline, with monotonic OOD improvement across both seeds.% exp_id: exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_ood_v2

The A$\to$B comparison isolates M2: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning. The B$\to$D comparison isolates M1: removing the information-loss ceiling enables OOD generalization. Both mechanisms are necessary to explain the full failure pattern. Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}(a) visualizes these trajectories.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_training_dynamics.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Training dynamics across factorial conditions (metric type indicated in legend). Cell~A collapses within 5 steps; Cell~B shows bounded learning; Cell~D improves monotonically. (b)~Window size $h$ vs.\ success rate (seed~1). $h{\leq}20$ bars use a 32-task in-distribution eval set; the episode-level bar uses a 134-task OOD set (dotted separator); magnitudes are not directly comparable across eval sets. A credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ separates collapse from recovery. Hatched bars indicate collapsed conditions.}
\label{fig:hsweep}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{1.7B results on ALFWorld (best checkpoint per seed, evaluated on held-out test sets). In-Dist: 32-task set; OOD: 134-task set. Cell~A reports best seed; Cell~B reports best checkpoint (step~5). Cell~D reports mean of two seeds (In-Dist range: 28.1--40.6; OOD range: 10.4--23.9).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Mechanisms} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & --- & 12.50 & 6.72 \\
Cell A ($h{=}2$, step) & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
Cell B ($h{=}2$, episode) & M1 only & 18.75 & 5.97 \\
Cell D (full, episode) & neither & \textbf{34.38} & \textbf{17.16} \\
\midrule
Dense reward & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
REINFORCE++ & neither & 12.50 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_v2, exp013f, gae_baseline_seed1
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional conditions.} Dense reward, REINFORCE++, and discount-factor sweep experiments all confirm that sign misattribution, not reward sparsity or discounting, drives the failure (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} Varying window size reveals a credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ that separates collapse from recovery (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}b; seed~1). Below this threshold, all conditions collapse to 0\%; above it, success rate increases monotonically with window size. This threshold is observed in a single seed; multi-seed verification is needed to confirm its robustness.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
\label{sec:results_hsweep}

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We operationalize local correctness (Definition~1) through episode outcome: in a successful episode, all windows with valid actions are labeled locally correct. The credit agreement rate (the fraction of such windows where GRPO's advantage sign is positive) is 52\% under step-independent training ($h{=}2$, $n{=}3{,}000$ windows), marginally above random chance. Advantage sign correlates near-perfectly with episode outcome but shows near-zero dependence on local action quality. Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}), these measurements confirm sign misattribution as the causal driver. This operationalization is a conservative approximation: successful episodes may contain suboptimal windows, making our label an upper bound on true local correctness. Even under this generous criterion, credit agreement remains near-random, strengthening the diagnosis.% exp_id: 7b_cell_a_h2

Under pure binary reward, sign-episode correlation reaches 100\%, directly confirming M2: advantage sign perfectly tracks episode outcome (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_misattribution}). Penalty reward partially breaks this by introducing within-group variance, but credit agreement remains near-random for local quality.% exp_id: binary_credit_agreement_iter0

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_sign_misattribution.pdf}
\caption{Sign-episode agreement rate under binary reward (penalty${=}0$) vs.\ penalty reward. Under binary reward, advantage sign perfectly tracks episode outcome (100\%, $n{=}157$), directly confirming M2. Penalty reward reduces this to 52.1\% ($n{=}3{,}000$), near random chance (dashed line).}
\label{fig:sign_misattribution}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO addresses the dual-mechanism failure, but training outcomes remain seed-dependent. We systematically evaluate $N{=}11$ random seeds under identical hyperparameters, revealing a $2{\times}2$ structure: \textit{training stability} (collapsed vs.\ non-collapsed) $\times$ \textit{OOD generalization} (stagnant vs.\ generalized).

We define \textbf{collapsed} seeds as those where in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak and remains below baseline for $\geq$3 consecutive steps. \textbf{Non-collapsed} seeds are the complement. Among non-collapsed seeds, we further distinguish \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set) from \textbf{stagnant} (not significant vs.\ SFT). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports results for each seed at its best-performing checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 11 seeds (134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$, best checkpoint per seed). Category: C=Collapsed, S=Non-collapsed (stagnant), G=Generalized (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT). Wilson 95\% CI. SFT baseline: 6.72\% (9/134). Seed2 achieves $p{=}0.03$ (Fisher exact, two-sided), falling outside the conservative $p{<}0.01$ threshold, and is classified as Stagnant. Step-5 results for all seeds in Appendix~\ref{app:step5_unified}.}
\label{tab:seed_variability}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Step} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{vs SFT} \\
\midrule
seed2048$^\ddagger$ & 5 & C & 8.21 & [4.6, 14.2] & NS \\
seed42$^\ddagger$ & 5 & C & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed8 & 5 & C & 11.94 & [7.5, 18.5] & NS \\
\midrule
seed200 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed300 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed100 & 5 & S & 11.19 & [6.9, 17.6] & NS \\
seed100 & 10 & S & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed2$^\ddagger$ & 15 & S & 15.67 & [10.5, 22.8] & $p{=}0.03$ \\
\midrule
seed1 & 5 & G$^\dagger$ & 23.88 & [17.4, 31.8] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed5 & 10 & G & 29.10 & [22.1, 37.3] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed6 & 15 & G & 29.10 & [22.2, 37.1] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed4 & 15 & G & 30.60 & [23.5, 38.7] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\begin{flushleft}
\small $^\dagger$ RAGEN built-in eval; checkpoint unavailable for re-evaluation. \\
\small $^\ddagger$ OOD evaluated via RAGEN built-in eval; not independently re-evaluated under D134 protocol. \\
\small All results are single-run evaluations at $\tau{=}0.4$; see Limitations for eval variance discussion.
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

Three distinct outcomes emerge (Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability}): roughly a quarter of seeds collapse with response length inflation (Appendix~\ref{app:response_length}), a third are stagnant with OOD performance indistinguishable from SFT, and a third generalize with significant OOD improvement. No collapsed seed generalizes, and training duration does not explain the gap between stagnant and generalized seeds. Stable training is necessary but not sufficient; the mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).% exp_id: exp029 multi-seed series

\subsection{Cross-Scale and Cross-Environment Verification}
\label{sec:cross_scale}

A single-seed experiment on Qwen3-8B provides preliminary evidence that both failure and fix persist at scale. Cell~A collapses within two steps; Cell~D reaches 90.3\% OOD, substantially exceeding the SFT baseline (full results in Appendix~\ref{app:8b_results}). Restricting context to $h{=}2$ produces a large M1 gap, confirming that information loss remains severe at scale. Single-seed due to memory constraints.% exp_id: 8b_cell_c_episode_v9d, 8b_v9d_ood_eval_v5
Step-independent training also collapses on ScienceWorld~\cite{wang2022scienceworld}: Cell~A drops to 0\% throughout all 15 steps. Restricting context to $h{=}2$ produces a comparable M1 gap to the 8B ALFWorld setting, suggesting the pathology is environment-general.% exp_id: scienceworld_grpo_cell_a, sw_info_loss_bjb1

